</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><math id="S4.T2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.T2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>, last layer. <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tools</span>: 200 ToolBench API mean activations. <span id="S4.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Match</span>: 200 non-tool topic queries with the same 200-tool prompt prefix. Negative <span id="S4.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Diff</span> means tool queries spread to more dimensions than non-tool queries; positive means they cluster more. <sup id="S4.T2.12" class="ltx_sup"><math id="S4.T2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math></sup>On Gemma 3 12B the non-tool baseline collapses (PC1<math id="S4.T2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math>72%). Diff 95% CIs (subsample bootstrap at 80% of <math id="S4.T2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>): Gemma 3 4B [<math id="S4.T2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>5, <math id="S4.T2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>2] (crosses zero), Gemma 3 12B [<math id="S4.T2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>51, <math id="S4.T2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>56], Qwen 3 4B [<math id="S4.T2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>18, <math id="S4.T2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>26], Llama 3.1 8B [<math id="S4.T2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>17, <math id="S4.T2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>10]; so Gemma 3 4B’s <math id="S4.T2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0 difference and the other three signs are all statistically robust. Source: <span id="S4.T2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">results/pca/matched_control_summary.json</span>, <span id="S4.T2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">matched_control_subsample.json</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We read this as: at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>, the simple “tool means use much fewer dimensions than random” claim plausibly reflects the long shared prompt prefix as much as anything tool-specific. The model-by-model picture is mixed: Gemma 3 4B looks indistinguishable from a non-tool baseline, Qwen 3 4B and Gemma 3 12B show tool queries spreading more than non-tool, and Llama 3.1 8B shows the reverse. The cleaner evidence for tool-specific structure comes from the K=15 controlled comparison (Appendix <a href="#A26" title="Appendix A.26 K=15 matched-prompt control ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.26</span></a>), the within-topic <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline probe (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, top-1 61–89%), and the steering and patching results, all of which probe tool identity directly rather than reading it off PCA dimensionality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The scaling curve is not perfectly smooth. At some values of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of dimensions dips before rising again. This happens because real-world APIs cluster by domain: adding another batch of finance tools does not require new dimensions (they fit into the “finance” region that already exists), but adding a batch of rare, unrelated tools does. The model’s internal space reflects the real semantic structure of the tool ecosystem, not a uniform spread (full scaling curve in Figure <a href="#A8.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix A.8 PCA dimensionality vs. tool count ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, Appendix).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also test whether tool descriptions (the short text explaining what each tool does) affect the compression. At small <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (below <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>150), descriptions make no difference: the tool name alone is enough. At larger <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, descriptions help the model compress better, but they also make the prompt longer. This creates a tradeoff: with descriptions, the model packs tools more tightly (36 vs. 48 dimensions at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>), but beyond about 750 tools the prompt gets so long that representations degrade. Without descriptions, the same model handles 2,000 tools with no collapse. Descriptions also help across languages: classifying Korean queries using English tool definitions goes from 60% to 93% when descriptions are added (Appendix <a href="#A22" title="Appendix A.22 Cross-lingual tool space alignment ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.22</span></a>). Full with-vs-without comparison in Appendix <a href="#A23" title="Appendix A.23 Does adding tool descriptions change the results? ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.23</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Tracing the decision from input to output</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now know that tool identity fits in a small space, but we have not yet asked <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> the model builds that representation, step by step from input to output. We use the term “circuit” loosely to mean the localized pathway responsible for tool selection, not a fully specified computational graph in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conmy et al. (2023)</a></cite>; we identify which components matter and how they contribute, but do not trace every edge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To trace the circuit, we need a way to decompose the model’s computation into interpretable parts. We use cross-layer transcoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ameisen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieberum et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: these are trained to break apart what each layer does into individual “features” (think of them as detectors for specific concepts, like “the user wants weather information” or “output a JSON brace”). By looking at which features light up for tool-calling prompts vs. non-tool prompts, we can identify which parts of the network are doing tool-specific work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this to Gemma 3 4B, a three-stage pipeline emerges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1 (early layers, L0–3):</span> we find 38 features that fire selectively for specific tools. These are genuinely tool-specific: when we test 3 non-tool prompts about weather, math, and translation <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> tool definitions, only 1 out of 38 features activates, and the one that does responds to a shared concept rather than a tool-specific one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2 (mid layers, L16–30):</span> attention heads lock onto tool-name and entity tokens (for example, L24 head 1 attends to “Tokyo” with weight 0.43).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3 (late layers, L30–33):</span> features handle JSON output formatting. These turn out to be generic code-generation features, shared with non-tool contexts rather than unique to tool calling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We replicate this on Qwen 3 4B (36 layers): both models show near-zero divergence at early layers, with steering becoming effective around the halfway point (Gemma: L17+, Qwen: L23+) and a spike at the final layer. The PCA subspace is comparably structured across families (Gemma: 92.5%, Qwen: 90.2% in 10 PCs; full comparison in Table <a href="#A10.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Appendix A.10 Cross-model circuit comparison ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, Appendix).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Does this circuit actually matter, or is it just correlation?</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The transcoder analysis tells us which features light up at each stage, but not whether the model actually <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">needs</em> them. To test this, we use activation patching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conmy et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: one at a time, we swap out each attention head’s output (and each MLP layer’s output) with the output from a different-tool query, and measure how much the model’s confidence in the correct tool drops. If a component matters, swapping it hurts; if it does not, the model barely notices. We repeat this across 10 tool pairs and average over every attention head and feedforward layer in the model (details in Appendix <a href="#A24" title="Appendix A.24 How we test which components matter (activation patching) ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.24</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The causal results line up with the three-stage structure we saw with transcoders. On Gemma 3 4B, just two attention heads in the middle of the network (L17 H0 and H1) matter more than every other head combined: swapping them drops the model’s confidence in the correct tool by 6.5 and 3.7 points respectively. The three stages contribute 17% (early), 37% (middle, dominated by L17), and 45% (late) of the total effect. On Qwen 3 4B, the split is similar (24%/33%/43%) but spread across more heads, consistent with Qwen having 4<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more heads. Summed across all components, attention totals 80% (Gemma) and 88% (Qwen) of the importance, with feedforward (MLP) layers contributing 20% and 12%. We are careful with this raw share, since these models have many more attention heads than MLPs (Gemma 3 4B: 272 heads vs. 34 MLPs; Qwen 3 4B: 1,152 heads vs. 36 MLPs). A fairer comparison is top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> heads versus top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> MLPs (Table <a href="#A24.T26" title="Table 26 ‣ Appendix A.24 How we test which components matter (activation patching) ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>): on Gemma 3 4B the top-3 heads carry 12.6 importance against 4.7 for the top-3 MLPs (a 2.7<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> gap), so a small number of attention heads dominate even on a per-component basis. On Qwen 3 4B the top-3 heads and top-3 MLPs are essentially tied (0.96 vs. 1.05), so the 88% raw attention share is almost entirely a counting artefact. The honest reading depends on the model: in Gemma 3 4B a few specific heads (L17 H0 and H1) carry the decision, while in Qwen 3 4B the load is split between attention heads and MLP layers at the top end. Either way, this differs from how prior work localizes factual recall like “Paris is the capital of France”, which is associated mostly with feedforward weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, but the contrast on Qwen is more about which specific components matter than a clean attention-versus-MLP split.</p>
</div>
