<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Argument schema adaptation. Steering changes the tool name <em id="S4.T5.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> restructures the argument keys/values to match the target schema, without schema information in the steering vector. Full JSON outputs in Appendix <a href="#A13" title="Appendix A.13 Full generation examples ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.13</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One simple explanation for the schema adaptation is autoregressive generation. Once the model has committed to a tool name, the rest of the output is just continuation from that name, and the training distribution of each tool’s arguments takes care of the schema. If this is right, we should be able to get the same schema-correct rate by forcing the tool name in the prompt, without touching the residual at all.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We checked this directly. For each of the 30 source-target pairs, we ran a prefill version that simply appended the target tool name to the prompt, let the model autoregress the rest, and measured schema correctness. Results are in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.4 The model also rewrites its arguments ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. On Gemma 3 4B and Qwen 3 4B, prefill matches activation steering exactly on schema correctness (73% vs 73% and 27% vs 27%). On Llama 3.1 8B, prefill edges out steering at 57% vs 43%, because steering only switches the tool name on 77% of the pairs there; conditioning on the pairs where the name does switch closes the gap (61% vs 57%). So the schema adaptation we see under steering is not carried by the steering vector itself; it is what autoregressive generation does once the right tool name is the first token.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A: base</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">B: prefill</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">C: steer</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">src schema</th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">tgt schema</th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">tgt schema</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Gemma 3 4B IT</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">87%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">73%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">73%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Qwen 3 4B</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">60%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">27%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">27%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Llama 3.1 8B IT</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">90%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">57%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">43%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Schema-correct rate across three conditions on the same 30 source<math id="S4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>target pairs. A: no intervention (expect source schema). B: prompt is prefilled with the target tool name and the model autoregresses the arguments. C: activation steering replaces B’s prefill. B and C land at the same rate on Gemma 3 4B and Qwen 3 4B; on Llama 3.1 8B the 14-point gap shrinks to 4 points once we restrict to pairs where steering actually switched the tool name, so the remaining difference reflects commit strength, not schema information in the steering vector.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The logit bias gap then makes sense from the same angle. Logit bias pushes the tool token from the output side and wins only about 20% of the time, so most of its low schema rate comes from the fact that the name never switches. Activation steering wins more often because it changes the model’s internal representation of which tool is being called, which locks in the name more reliably. The steering vector encodes tool identity, not tool interface; the interface follows for free once the name is in place.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also probed what steering can and cannot do.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Argument steering</em> fails: redirecting “Tokyo” to “Paris” while keeping the same tool gives 0/30 (the tool is preserved, but the argument never changes). This makes sense. Picking a tool is choosing from a short, fixed list, so a few directions suffice. Argument values are pulled from the query on the fly, not from a fixed menu. <em id="S4.SS4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Format steering</em> (JSON vs. plain text) also fails (0/10), and <em id="S4.SS4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-family transfer</em> gives 0% (Gemma vectors on Qwen), suggesting each model learns its own subspace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multi-turn steering</em> shows a mixed picture. On a synthetic two-turn setup where the second turn still asks to pick from a fixed menu, applying single-turn vectors at the second turn gives 100% at 4B+. On <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline, a realistic multi-turn agent benchmark, results are much less clean once we compare against fresh, non-crashing baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Across five 4B–14B models (Gemma 3 4B / 12B, Qwen 3 4B / 8B / 14B) with <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}20</annotation></semantics></math> tasks each, matched-context steering ranges from <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>30 to <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>10 percentage points against the fresh baseline, with no consistent direction. Gemma 4B gains 10 points at L25 but loses 5 at L33. Gemma 12B at L47 drops 30 points below baseline (25% vs 55%). Qwen 4B gains 5 points, while Qwen 8B and 14B lose 20 and 10 respectively. With only 20 tasks per cell, the binomial 95% CI on a single <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>10pp delta is roughly <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>22pp, so individual deltas are not all statistically distinguishable from zero. What is clear is that the consistent positive direction we see in single-turn, fixed-menu evaluation goes away here. We return to this in the limitations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>When does this structure appear?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If tool selection has a linear, intervenable representation, a natural question is whether this is always the case or whether it develops at some point. We run the same steering experiments across all 15 models (Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). At 270M, the model cannot tell tools apart at all (27% steering, roughly random). By 1B, 5-tool switching works perfectly (100%) but 15-tool is only partial (43%), so the structure is emerging but not yet robust. At 4B and above, all three families reach <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>83% on 15 tools, and the trend keeps going: Gemma 3 12B hits 97%, Gemma 3 27B and Qwen 3 8B reach 100%. One anomaly: Gemma 3 27B scores lower than 4B on <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-air (77% vs. 94%), but the confidence intervals overlap ([59,88] vs. [84,98] at <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}30</annotation></semantics></math>), and the gap may come from the longer context at 27B diluting the steering vector’s relative strength. Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3 Can we exploit this structure to control tool selection? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> gives the full picture across five evaluation dimensions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
