<td id="S4.T8.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" colspan="5"><span id="S4.T8.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.0pt;"><img src="2605.07990v1/figures/logos/llama.png" id="S4.T8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:12/12;" width="12" height="12" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span> <span id="S4.T8.2.9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama 3.1<sup id="S4.T8.2.9.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T8.2.10" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">
<td id="S4.T8.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T8.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">8B</span></td>
<td id="S4.T8.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T8.2.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">6.8 (L19)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T8.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T8.2.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">95</span></td>
<td id="S4.T8.2.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" colspan="2">—</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>SAE sharpening (full depth in Table <a href="#A15.T19" title="Table 19 ‣ Appendix A.15 Full SAE feature analysis ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>). <sup id="S4.T8.6" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>270M base has <em id="S4.T8.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more</em> specific features yet both fail at steering. <sup id="S4.T8.8" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>Goodfire (65K vs 16K).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feature evolution across scales</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">That covers the circuit in one model. Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> also showed that the linear structure is absent at 270M and emerges by 1B. What changes at the feature level?</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To answer this, we use sparse autoencoders (SAEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieberum et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajamanoharan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. SAEs serve a similar purpose to transcoders but can be applied at any layer. They decompose the residual stream into a sparse set of features, which lets us count how many of those features are tool-specific and how many are shared across tools. Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> tracks these counts across scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At 270M-IT, essentially no tool-specific features appear at early layers (L5: 0 specific), and even at L15 there are only 16 (specific-to-shared ratio 0.3), consistent with the steering failure we saw (27%). The 270M <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">base</em> model is the surprising part: it actually has <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more</em> tool-specific features (77 at L15, ratio 2.4), but steering still fails (20%). At sub-1B scale, tool-related patterns exist in the activations without driving the model’s output, i.e. they are statistical regularities without causal influence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By 1B-IT, things change. The specific-to-shared ratio rises from 0.1 (L7) to 1.3 (L13) to <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10.4</span> (L17, 125 features), a sharp peak at 65% depth. At 12B-IT, sharpening spans the full network: 0.1 (L12) <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 1.6 (L24) <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 2.3 (L31) <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 4.1 (L41). The simplest reading is that instruction tuning organizes scattered features into functional pathways rather than creating new ones: fewer but causally connected features (1B-IT ratio 1.3) outperform many disconnected ones (270M-base ratio 2.4). One caveat: SAE feature counts measure what the residual stream encodes, not whether those features are causally connected to the output. The 270M paradox shows the two can come apart, so the “functional pathways” reading at 1B+ relies on the activation-patching results in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Tracing the decision from input to output ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, not on SAE counts alone, for its causal claim.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The role of instruction tuning</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SAE analysis points to instruction tuning as a key factor. We can test this directly by comparing base and IT models (Table <a href="#A16.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ Appendix A.16 Base vs instruction-tuned comparison ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> in Appendix). IT consistently outperforms base with large effect sizes (Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math> at every scale). At 1B, the base model cannot steer at all (20%, chance level); by 27B, it reaches 65% vs. 100% for IT. But here is the interesting part: base models <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">do</em> land in a similar PCA spectrum to IT (89–93% top-10 variance). The geometry is there, but it does not translate into effective steering. The short answer is alignment with the output layer: the linear representation exists, but it does not point in a direction that changes what the model actually outputs (we measure this directly in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and find that IT models have 2<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher alignment between the steering direction and the unembedding row of the target tool’s first token). The SAE analysis echoes the same gap from the feature side: at 1B L13, the base has 3<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer tool-specific features (11 vs. 36) and 1.5<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more shared ones (40 vs. 27).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two robustness checks complete the mechanistic picture. First, steering is robust to the number of tools: with distractors added to the prompt (up to <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}150</annotation></semantics></math>), accuracy on the 5 core tools stays at <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>100%, and when steering among randomly sampled tools from all <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, every 4B+ model stays above 90% (Table <a href="#A6.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix A.6 Scaling results ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Second, sweeping the steering scale <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> from 0.1 to 3.0 across 15 models (Table <a href="#A17.T21" title="Table 21 ‣ Appendix A.17 Steering strength calibration ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> in Appendix) reveals a sharp phase transition at <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\approx 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\approx 0.6</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math>: below this, steering does nothing; above it, accuracy jumps to 95–100% and stays there even at <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}3.0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, everything above is correlational: we see features and attention patterns, but are the late layers actually <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">necessary</em> for the decision? To check, we use attribution patching: we measure how much the model’s tool prediction changes when we replace each layer’s activations with those from a different query (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Does this circuit actually matter, or is it just correlation? ‣ 4.2 Tracing the decision from input to output ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The layers where swapping causes the biggest change are the ones that matter most for the decision. Peak importance occurs at 89–98% depth across all 4B+ models (Qwen 3 1.7B is an outlier at 71%). Base models have the same peak layer but 3–12<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> lower importance (e.g., Gemma 3 27B: 1.8 vs. 14.2 IT), suggesting that instruction tuning amplifies the tool-selection signal rather than just shifting its location.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Why should we expect linearity?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before discussing the intuition, we want to address a concern that has been in the background: is our argument circular? We claim tool identity is linear, and our most visible evidence is that linear steering works. But the linearity conclusion rests on three <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independent</em> lines of evidence, not just steering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Activation patching identifies the causal components without using any steering vectors. Random vectors at the same norm as our steering vectors give 0% switch rate, so the direction matters and not just the size of the perturbation. The within-topic <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline probe (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) reads tool identity off the residual stream at top-1 61–89% even when all 14 tools share the airline domain, so the linear signal cannot be just topic injection. We note that the PCA dimensionality results at <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}200</annotation></semantics></math> do <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> survive matched-prompt controls (Sections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A26" title="Appendix A.26 K=15 matched-prompt control ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.26</span></a>) and do not provide independent evidence of tool-specific structure. Steering is the most dramatic demonstration, but the linearity conclusion rests on the interventional and probe-based evidence above, not on raw PCA dimensionality counts.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Are we just injecting topics, not switching tools?</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural worry about what the steering vector actually captures. When we compute the direction from weather to search, the queries used to build the weather mean are about weather, and the queries for search are about searching. So the vector might just encode “shift the topic from weather stuff to search stuff,” and the model switches its output because the context now feels more search-like, not because we flipped a genuine tool-routing signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Five pieces of evidence say otherwise. First, and most directly: when we steer from weather to search, the model does not just swap the tool name. It rewrites the entire argument structure, changing <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">location: "Tokyo"</span> to <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">query: "weather in Tokyo"</span> (Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.4 The model also rewrites its arguments ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The model somehow knows what parameters the target tool expects. Topic injection cannot explain that; the vector has to carry information about the tool’s interface, not just its subject matter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, there is a telling failure case that cuts against the topic explanation. Four of our tools share the prefix <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">get_</span> (get_weather, get_stock, get_news, get_directions). Their topics are completely different (weather, finance, news, navigation), so if steering were topic-based, these should be the easiest to tell apart. Instead, they are the hardest: the model confuses them because they share a first token. The bottleneck is the tool’s name in the tokenizer, not its meaning, which is exactly what you would expect if the vector targets the output token, not the semantic theme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Third, steering works at 77–100% even when the prompt contains only bare tool names with no descriptions. The query still says “What is the weather in Tokyo” (a weather topic), but the model outputs search. The topic of the input has not changed; only the tool choice has.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Fourth, our activation patching (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Tracing the decision from input to output ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) localizes the tool decision in a few specific attention heads (like L17 H0/H1 in Gemma 3 4B), not in the feedforward layers that prior work links to memorized associations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. If steering worked by injecting a memorized topic, you would expect MLP layers (the part that stores “weather = rain, temperature, forecast”) to be the heaviest hitters per component. On Gemma the strongest individual contributors are heads, not MLPs; on Qwen the top heads and top MLPs are roughly tied (Table <a href="#A24.T26" title="Table 26 ‣ Appendix A.24 How we test which components matter (activation patching) ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>), so the contrast with factual recall is partial rather than absolute.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Fifth, we directly test whether the signal is tool-level or just topic-level. All 14 <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline tools live in one topic (airline service) but do different things: book a reservation, cancel one, update flights, search for a direct flight, transfer the user to a human agent, and so on. If we can tell these apart from the model’s internal state, it cannot be because we are picking up “this is an airline query”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We train a logistic-regression classifier on the residual stream at the last prompt token, the position right before the model would emit a tool name, and ask it to predict which tool the model will call. Keeping tools that the model calls at least ten times leaves <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> labels depending on the model. Across five 4B–14B models, top-1 accuracy is 61–89% and top-5 is 96–100% (full numbers in Appendix <a href="#A29" title="Appendix A.29 Within-topic probe details ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.29</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">How to read those numbers. If the internal state carried only the topic and nothing tool-specific, different tools would look the same to the classifier and accuracy would drop to chance, which is 11–20% for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>. It does not. Two sanity checks on the pipeline: training on randomly shuffled labels gives 8–25%, near chance, so the classifier is not memorizing. Replacing the activations with Gaussian noise of the same shape gives 18–42%, above chance because with so few training points a linear classifier can latch onto noise that happens to correlate with labels in a particular split. Our real probe still sits 29–57 percentage points above this noi